===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} and {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|bailif|lang=fro}} (plural {{term|bailis|lang=fro}}), probably from {{etyl|LL.|en}} reconstructed as {{term|Appendix:Latin/baiulivus|*bāiulivus|[[castellan]]|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|baiulus||[[porter]]; [[steward]]|lang=la}}. As a translation of foreign titles, via {{etyl|fr}} {{term|bailli|lang=fr}}, {{etyl|sco}} {{term|bailie|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|nl}} {{term|baljuw|lang=nl}}, [[&c.]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|bāʹ-lĭf}}, {{IPA|/ˈbeɪlɪf/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[officer]] of the [[court]], ''[[particularly]]'':
## {{context|historical}} The [[Norman]] [[term]] for a [[reeve]], {{context|specifically}} the [[chief]] [[officer]] [[executing]] the [[decision]]s of [[any]] [[English]] [[court of law|court]] in the [[period]] [[follow]]ing the [[Norman Conquest]] or [[executing]] the [[decision]]s of [[lower]] [[court of law|court]]s in the [[late]] [[medieval]] and [[early modern]] [[period]].
## {{context|law enforcement}} A [[law enforcement]] [[officer]] under a [[sheriff]] [[responsible]] for [[executing]] [[warrant]]s and [[court order]]s; a [[huissier de justice]], the [[equivalent]] [[position]] in [[various]] [[Europe]]an [[countries]].
## A [[bumbailiff]]: a [[deputy]] bailiff [[charge]]d with [[debt]] [[collection]].
## {{context|UK}} A [[high bailiff]]: an [[officer]] of the [[county court]]s [[appoint]]ed by the [[judge]] and [[removable]] by the [[Lord Chancellor]].
## {{context|US}} A [[generic]] [[term]] for [[any]] [[law enforcement]] [[officer]] [[charge]]d with [[courtroom]] [[security]] and [[order]].
# A [[public]] [[administrator]], ''[[particularly]]:''
## {{context|obsolete}} A [[king]]'s [[man]]: a [[generic]] [[term]] for [[any]] [[officer]] [[nominate]]d by the [[English]] [[Crown]].
## {{context|historical}} The [[chief]] [[officer]] of a [[hundred]] in [[medieval]] [[England]].
## The [[title]] of the [[mayor]] of [[certain]] [[English]] [[town]]s.
## The [[title]] of the [[castellan]] of [[certain]] [[royal]] [[castle]]s in [[England]].
## The [[chief justice]] and [[president]] of the [[legislature]] on [[Jersey]] and [[Guernsey]] in the [[Channel Islands]].
##* {{quote-web|date=2011-06-29|title=The Bailiff of Jersey|site=States Assembly|url=http://www.statesassembly.gov.je/about/history/Pages/BailiffJersey.aspx|accessdate=2013-03-03|passage=The '''Bailiff''' of Jersey is the President of the States and acts as Speaker of the Assembly in the Westminster tradition. He is responsible for the orderly conduct of the States Assembly and its business. As Presiding Officer he has the right of speech – which is mainly exercised for ensuring the orderly conduct of the proceedings – but he cannot vote.}}
## The [[High Bailiff]] of the [[Isle of Man]].
## {{context|obsolete}} A [[bailie]]: an [[alderman]] in [[certain]] [[Scottish]] [[town]]s.
## {{context|historical}} An [[appointee]] of the [[French]] [[king]] [[administer]]ing [[certain]] [[district]]s of [[northern]] [[France]] in the [[Middle Ages]].
## {{context|historical}} A [[head]] of a [[district]] ("[[bailiwick]]") of the [[Knights Hospitaller]]; a [[head]] of [[one]] of the [[national]] [[association]]s ("[[tongue]]s") of the [[Hospitallers]]' [[headquarters]] on [[Rhodes]] or [[Malta]].
## {{context|historical}} A [[landvogt]] in the [[medieval]] [[German]] [[state]]s.
# A [[private]] [[administrator]], ''[[particularly]]''
{{anchor|Steward}}
## {{context|historical}} A [[steward]]: the [[manager]] of a [[medieval]] [[manor]] [[charge]]d with [[collect]]ing its [[rent]]s, [[&c.]]
## {{context|historical}} An [[overseer]]: a [[supervisor]] of [[tenant]] [[farmer]]s, [[serf]]s, or [[slave]]s, [[usually]] as [[part]] of [[his]] [[role]] as [[steward]] ([[#Steward|''see above'']]).
##* {{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}} |chapter=19 |title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp] |passage=Nothing was too small to receive attention, if a supervising eye could suggest improvements likely to conduce to the common welfare. Mr. Gordon Burnage, for instance, personally visited dust-bins and back premises, accompanied by a sort of village '''bailiff''', going his round like a commanding officer doing billets.}}
# {{context|UK|slang}} [[any|Any]] [[debt collector]], [[regardless]] of [[his]] or [[her]] [[official]] [[status]].

